WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.380
Adventures of Lolo, overcome the challenges of each room in the great Devil's Castle.

00:00:20.400 --> 00:00:27.840
And welcome to NEStalgia, a chronological exploration of every NES game released in North America.

00:00:28.060 --> 00:00:29.800
I'm Mike. I'm Sean.

00:00:30.440 --> 00:00:32.700
I'm Joe. And I'm Sam.

00:00:33.420 --> 00:00:38.220
We are playing quite possibly the first feel-good game of the NES.

00:00:38.480 --> 00:00:39.860
Am I wrong in saying something like that?

00:00:40.920 --> 00:00:41.760
You're not wrong.

00:00:42.000 --> 00:00:43.540
It's an odd thing to say.

00:00:44.360 --> 00:00:45.960
Come on. This is like such a...

00:00:45.960 --> 00:00:47.700
Look at Lolo. He's such a cutie.

00:00:47.900 --> 00:00:50.960
And the puzzles are like, you know, they're pretty easy to start.

00:00:50.980 --> 00:00:53.700
And they get progressively maybe a little more challenging or what have you.

00:00:53.700 --> 00:00:55.120
But everything's fun.

00:00:55.280 --> 00:00:58.820
The song is cute, even if it is just one song.

00:00:59.600 --> 00:01:01.780
You know, like everything about the game is just pleasant.

00:01:02.600 --> 00:01:05.500
I'll say that I definitely agree that this is a feel-good game.

00:01:05.520 --> 00:01:08.680
I just don't want to commit yet to saying that it was the first one without having...

00:01:08.680 --> 00:01:10.580
Without thinking back through the library.

00:01:11.200 --> 00:01:12.860
I feel like we might have had another feel-good game.

00:01:12.860 --> 00:01:14.260
Yeah, what about Gumshoe?

00:01:16.240 --> 00:01:19.100
It sounds like you have a personal attachment to this game, Mike.

00:01:19.660 --> 00:01:25.680
Well, that's a great segue, Sean, because this was one of the few NES games I owned as a child.

00:01:25.960 --> 00:01:27.980
So I know this one pretty well.

00:01:28.260 --> 00:01:32.680
That song is something that I will just think of from time to time while I'm running.

00:01:32.780 --> 00:01:34.260
And I don't like that one bit.

00:01:34.260 --> 00:01:36.420
That it gets stuck in my head like that.

00:01:36.720 --> 00:01:38.860
You know, it's not even like an earworm thing, right?

00:01:38.920 --> 00:01:40.980
Like it's not something that should be stuck in your head.

00:01:41.060 --> 00:01:44.180
But you play the game enough and it just winds up being there.

00:01:44.420 --> 00:01:45.800
No, I totally get that.

00:01:46.020 --> 00:01:48.520
That sounds like something that would happen to me.

00:01:49.000 --> 00:01:51.040
Joe was singing it to us in the pre-show.

00:01:51.660 --> 00:01:53.220
I was. I've been singing it all week.

00:01:53.380 --> 00:01:55.040
Can you do it for us?

00:01:55.240 --> 00:01:56.380
No, thank you.

00:01:57.020 --> 00:01:59.600
Oh. Sorry, you'll have to play the game.

00:01:59.700 --> 00:02:03.140
I don't want to, you know, give people a reason to not play it if they're like,

00:02:03.140 --> 00:02:04.120
Oh, I already heard the music.

00:02:05.540 --> 00:02:08.720
That's one of my top things is like I only play a game for the soundtrack.

00:02:09.020 --> 00:02:10.060
I'm like, oh, shoot.

00:02:10.160 --> 00:02:14.800
Like if only there was like a way to hear all these songs without having to play these boring video games.

00:02:15.040 --> 00:02:18.100
Right, right. So Adventures of Lolo.

00:02:18.320 --> 00:02:24.460
Top down puzzle game where the goal in each room is to collect all these like cute little hearts.

00:02:25.320 --> 00:02:30.940
And then if you collect all those hearts, it makes like a chest appear that makes an exit appear.

00:02:31.260 --> 00:02:32.560
So, or sorry, the chest opens.

00:02:32.560 --> 00:02:34.920
The chest is always in the room, but then it opens once you collect all the hearts.

00:02:35.220 --> 00:02:39.200
And then from there, you can go to the exit once the chest has been collected.

00:02:39.720 --> 00:02:46.640
So to collect these hearts, Lolo moves blocks and he turns enemies into what I'm going to guess are like eggs.

00:02:47.020 --> 00:02:48.160
Right. That looks like eggs.

00:02:48.500 --> 00:02:49.620
They call it eggs.

00:02:49.760 --> 00:02:52.460
Yeah. Cool. So they can break out of the eggs too.

00:02:53.420 --> 00:02:57.920
Yeah. And like, you know, I think when you lay out that those are the rules of the game, right?

00:02:57.920 --> 00:03:01.680
Like you, you put them in eggs, you move blocks, you collect hearts.

00:03:01.860 --> 00:03:07.920
It sounds pretty straightforward, but there's some, there's some decent puzzle action going on here.

00:03:08.000 --> 00:03:14.060
Right. I'm not saying it's Solomon's key levels of difficulty, but like there's a lot of options.

00:03:14.060 --> 00:03:15.360
Oh, see.

00:03:15.360 --> 00:03:17.780
Based on moving blocks and dealing with enemies.

00:03:18.040 --> 00:03:21.180
Right. Did we feel like this was, this fits the puzzle game genre?

00:03:21.500 --> 00:03:22.360
Oh, for sure.

00:03:22.540 --> 00:03:32.760
This is, it's got lots of different mechanics going on with, in terms of like what different enemies do as like obstacles.

00:03:32.760 --> 00:03:36.520
It's a, it's the, it's the quintessential puzzle game.

00:03:36.960 --> 00:03:43.460
Yeah. I I've heard that this game actually helped define the puzzle genre with those block puzzles specifically.

00:03:44.640 --> 00:03:53.300
And that it might be a reason why we see them come up in more games and in more like Zelda dungeons, you get those block puzzles a lot.

00:03:53.480 --> 00:03:56.440
And so this may be a precursor to all of that.

00:03:56.620 --> 00:03:57.520
Makes sense. Yeah.

00:03:58.000 --> 00:04:00.000
I think that they did a lot with it.

00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:01.740
And I think it's funny that you mentioned Solomon's key.

00:04:01.860 --> 00:04:11.880
Cause I actually thought that this was an improvement on the puzzle mechanics of not the puzzle mechanics specifically, but just puzzle design from over Solomon's key.

00:04:14.520 --> 00:04:19.020
I don't know what you guys thought about that, but I felt like Solomon's key was good, but had, had some, some flaws.

00:04:19.120 --> 00:04:24.780
I felt like this, every puzzle felt like there were first of all, increasing level of difficulty.

00:04:24.940 --> 00:04:27.300
And second of all, you know, made you think about things in different ways.

00:04:27.300 --> 00:04:29.940
Even after you thought like, all right, you got the game down.

00:04:30.140 --> 00:04:31.800
It was, it was, it was a nice progression.

00:04:32.100 --> 00:04:34.980
I'd say each, each puzzle in this game.

00:04:35.320 --> 00:04:39.720
Uh, and again, like the, the, the basics never really changed.

00:04:39.920 --> 00:04:44.780
It just adds more layers of, uh, of, um, like enemy combinations.

00:04:44.780 --> 00:04:48.860
Uh, but like every, every, every puzzle feels fresh.

00:04:49.220 --> 00:04:59.300
And, uh, it sort of reminded me of like, cause I never really played puzzle games until like the indie, like all the indie games were sort of coming out like braid.

00:05:00.000 --> 00:05:09.580
Um, and, uh, this kind of reminded me of like starting to really get into puzzle platformers, puzzle, top down puzzle games.

00:05:09.580 --> 00:05:17.020
Uh, I think it's a, uh, this was just really good at doing that regardless of when this game came out.

00:05:17.560 --> 00:05:22.180
It really helps that all of the action for each level is just one screen.

00:05:22.640 --> 00:05:25.800
Um, think of it like one room in a Zelda dungeon, right?

00:05:26.300 --> 00:05:37.920
Uh, I, I think if you actually had like a gigantic map for each level where you couldn't see what was going on throughout the entirety, um, of the puzzle, that would hurt this game a lot.

00:05:37.920 --> 00:05:46.840
So it was very smart of them to make these smaller contained puzzles, um, that again, you know, still have, still have a lot of unique characteristics around them.

00:05:46.940 --> 00:05:55.440
But I had to say like, that seems like an easy mistake to, to, to think like, oh, the puzzle should be bigger than just a one screen thing.

00:05:55.580 --> 00:06:01.820
And this was the time where like smaller bursts of gameplay, uh, made it more fun, right?

00:06:01.820 --> 00:06:05.020
Like it, it, no challenge lasts too long.

00:06:05.480 --> 00:06:15.080
Yeah. It's sort of like, um, like with portal, like each, uh, like each, uh, little puzzle is just a room in portal.

00:06:15.080 --> 00:06:27.000
And you don't have to, I think later in the game, you start to think about, uh, larger scale, uh, thought, uh, problem solving, but everything that you need is right in front of you.

00:06:27.260 --> 00:06:36.940
And it's, uh, it's easy to sort of get into a more problem solving, abstract way of thinking when, uh, your scope is smaller.

00:06:43.280 --> 00:06:51.060
In terms of enemies though, you know, cause there are a bunch and, uh, you know, some are like completely harmless, won't even move.

00:06:51.400 --> 00:07:06.920
Right. And then there's other ones that, you know, I'm sure the manual explains it to you, but if you didn't know better, you wouldn't think that that's how you have to like handle them at first sight, you know, and you have to kind of like fail once or maybe a few times and then figure out how to do it.

00:07:06.920 --> 00:07:10.920
Like, oh, that's how these enemies behave or something like that.

00:07:11.140 --> 00:07:15.740
I'm not sure if that's like a problem, you know, that's just like a way that you, you learn to play the game.

00:07:16.240 --> 00:07:21.420
Uh, but was the, you know, like were the enemy variety, it wasn't enough.

00:07:21.860 --> 00:07:30.120
Did it feel like that was adding a lot to the gameplay or should they have tried to, um, you know, to make combat based enemies too?

00:07:31.480 --> 00:07:34.800
I'm glad that they didn't try to make too much combat based enemies.

00:07:34.800 --> 00:07:45.540
I think that like a problem we've had with other puzzle games so far, maybe this is personal preference, is that, that they, they didn't just commit to being a puzzle game.

00:07:45.540 --> 00:07:50.780
Like there, there would be, there'd be timers or there would be enemies you'd have to fight while playing it.

00:07:50.860 --> 00:07:57.240
It didn't really give you the time to kind of like, to kind of sit and kind of get absorbed in the puzzle, which this game does a pretty good job at.

00:07:57.320 --> 00:07:59.740
There's a couple of times where there's like, there's some enemies that chase you.

00:07:59.740 --> 00:08:06.520
Um, but like it's infrequent enough that it feels like it's a nice, like refreshing change of pace to that puzzle compared to others.

00:08:06.980 --> 00:08:17.360
Um, but I'm glad that they didn't, that the, the enemies are all for the most part, part of the puzzle, either something you have to avoid or sometimes something you have to use to help avoid other enemies or help you get to something.

00:08:18.140 --> 00:08:19.440
Yeah, I, I agree.

00:08:19.700 --> 00:08:26.320
I was going to say that I don't really see, uh, these enemies as enemies, more so just obstacles.

00:08:26.600 --> 00:08:40.220
And I think if they added combat to the mix, that would really confuse me because they have a pretty, um, uh, they have a pretty good gig going, uh, with what they have.

00:08:40.220 --> 00:08:53.720
I think that if you had anything beyond just like the little, uh, gun that, that turns them into eggs, it would, uh, it would sort of mess with the purity of, of what they have here.

00:08:53.940 --> 00:09:00.440
Yeah, I agree. I don't have much to add, but I like that it remains true to a puzzle game.

00:09:00.440 --> 00:09:05.600
Um, and, and like the, you know, the elements of all that stuff that we're talking about, right?

00:09:05.720 --> 00:09:14.560
Whether it's the enemies or, uh, you know, transforming them into the eggs, like all of everything that you're dealing with per stage is in a limited supply.

00:09:14.600 --> 00:09:22.260
So you do have to think about like which enemy you will turn into an egg, you know, like if, you know, there are some puzzles that depend on that.

00:09:22.360 --> 00:09:30.040
Like if you do the wrong one, you're just, you've kind of failed the puzzle, but the blocks too, it's like you can move the blocks in a way that you can't get them out of.

00:09:30.040 --> 00:09:33.100
And therefore you'll have to think about that too and restart the level.

00:09:33.420 --> 00:09:39.360
Uh, thankfully there is a like, you know, kill button to, uh, to restart the level.

00:09:39.520 --> 00:09:41.080
Like to the, when you realize you're locked in.

00:09:41.220 --> 00:09:59.940
Yeah. But I, I thought that was an interesting, um, you know, an interesting take on your, on your resources and just on puzzle solving in general that like every element of this game, you know, whether it's the immovable objects, the blocks, the enemies, or the, your, your power.

00:10:00.040 --> 00:10:04.200
Or the ability to turn to eggs, all that stuff counts towards the puzzle aspect of it.

00:10:04.360 --> 00:10:08.160
So you have to think in a, um, in a variety of ways.

00:10:08.280 --> 00:10:10.900
Like you can't just like, you can't take it as you go.

00:10:11.020 --> 00:10:17.380
You have to look at the whole puzzle because you might do something that's going to make the puzzle later on unsolvable.

00:10:18.220 --> 00:10:26.140
Yeah. I guess that that's sort of, I was sort of, uh, wrestling with the idea that I didn't really like the fact that there were lives.

00:10:26.440 --> 00:10:37.720
Um, and I think that the puzzle, the puzzles themselves were enough to keep you engaged in like there, they are, um, uh, they're challenging enough.

00:10:37.720 --> 00:10:46.700
Like once you start to get further into the game that like, you don't need a, uh, an artificial way of, of extending the game.

00:10:46.700 --> 00:11:03.800
Um, but it gets, I guess it does sort of, uh, it does give the, uh, the player more of a reason to think more slowly instead of, uh, instead of just like, well, I'll just reset real quick.

00:11:03.800 --> 00:11:08.340
Um, that, that the fact that there is a fail state that would make you lose progress.

00:11:08.820 --> 00:11:15.300
Um, so I guess I don't hate it, but it is still something that I was sort of, uh, it was something that I had to grapple with.

00:11:15.440 --> 00:11:33.780
Yeah. I kind of, I sort of felt the same way too, but now just you explaining it right now actually kind of made me change my mind a little bit where I do think that it's, it's, it's not a bad thing to have to encourage people to like stop and really look at everything and think like, I think that's, what's fun about this game is that like the puzzles are designed in a way that you could,

00:11:33.800 --> 00:11:39.860
solve them, you know, it teaches you what everything does in a, in a great way that you could solve them before you even touch your controller.

00:11:40.040 --> 00:11:45.500
But if you, if you're really, really careful, but like that can get confusing cause you might, you might miss things and stuff.

00:11:45.540 --> 00:11:48.740
It just, it just makes it, um, I don't know.

00:11:48.780 --> 00:11:49.780
It's like a game about planning.

00:11:49.980 --> 00:11:59.100
And, and, and I think that, yeah, if you were able to just constantly use the, uh, the like reset button, you could just, you could just guess and check until you solve it.

00:11:59.800 --> 00:12:07.560
Yeah. But a part of me, you know, like appreciates the simplicity of it, you know, like of, of everything in the game.

00:12:07.680 --> 00:12:14.780
The fact that like Lolo is pretty much just like helpless, you know, like he just needs to figure out how to solve the puzzles without anything.

00:12:15.800 --> 00:12:22.480
But there's, there's some cool moments, you know, in the game where like you turn, uh, an enemy into an egg and push them into the water.

00:12:22.480 --> 00:12:30.520
And then like that egg becomes like a moving, uh, object in the water that you can use to like become bridges and stuff, you know, and stuff like that.

00:12:30.520 --> 00:12:31.860
So this way you can cross the water.

00:12:32.720 --> 00:12:34.840
Those are all interesting moments and stuff.

00:12:35.100 --> 00:12:48.760
I kind of wish that there was, there was more surprises like that where, uh, you know, these limited resources that you have actually turn out to like, you know, the, they become a consequence of what you do with them.

00:12:48.760 --> 00:12:55.860
So to just to use that, uh, the egg analogy again, when you push that in the water, that's not something like you could do that on accident the first time.

00:12:55.980 --> 00:12:57.680
Right. And be like, Oh, now the egg floats.

00:12:57.800 --> 00:13:01.020
Like I thought it would just like go away or like that would kill the enemy or something.

00:13:01.040 --> 00:13:03.040
Like now you have to think with that in mind.

00:13:03.260 --> 00:13:06.140
I was kind of hoping for more moments like that throughout the game.

00:13:06.140 --> 00:13:07.360
Yeah, I get that.

00:13:07.580 --> 00:13:16.360
Uh, I'm always a fan of, uh, of there being more like things that do more than one thing.

00:13:16.480 --> 00:13:21.760
And I know that there's a better way to describe it than that, but I feel like you, you, that's just what you were describing.

00:13:21.760 --> 00:13:38.120
Um, uh, but I, I do think that this game still has enough that I would qualify it as a game that, that, that does well with the amount of sort of interconnectedness, uh, of all of it.

00:13:38.220 --> 00:13:42.340
I, but you're right. There, there's always, I'm always a fan of more stuff like that.

00:13:43.100 --> 00:13:50.040
I think one moment that kind of strikes out like that is you, you can move the blocks around.

00:13:50.620 --> 00:13:59.220
Uh, but they, instead of moving it in like full, like when you push a block, it doesn't just move a whole block space.

00:13:59.400 --> 00:14:00.500
It moves half a block space.

00:14:00.700 --> 00:14:09.140
And sometimes that becomes useful because you can block two things if it's on a half of, um, if it's like, Oh yeah.

00:14:09.520 --> 00:14:10.320
You know what I'm saying?

00:14:10.460 --> 00:14:12.200
Like if it's like not right on the grid.

00:14:12.340 --> 00:14:14.440
You know, it's like in between two points.

00:14:14.640 --> 00:14:26.400
Yeah. If it's in between two points and there's enemies or something that you need to block on both of those two points, then one block positioned correctly does the work of two blocks.

00:14:26.740 --> 00:14:32.380
So I think that's like another one of those like aha moments that Mike was talking about.

00:14:32.820 --> 00:14:42.080
Yeah. No, those, I mean, I, I, you guys, I agree that there could be more of those, but also like those, that itch that those aha moments scratch for me.

00:14:42.080 --> 00:14:47.920
Or like just very, that very satisfying feeling of like realizing something does something else.

00:14:47.920 --> 00:14:51.640
I got that feeling just beating half of these puzzles.

00:14:51.640 --> 00:14:56.200
I mean, like they were, some of them, you know, you'd be like, okay, wait, this doesn't make any sense.

00:14:56.300 --> 00:14:58.320
And then like when it clicks, it's really satisfying.

00:14:58.700 --> 00:15:06.280
And I compare that sometimes a little bit to like early on when we played Wrecking Crew where there were some puzzles that were like, ah, yeah, I figured that without satisfying.

00:15:06.500 --> 00:15:09.760
And there were a lot where it was just like, okay, they, you gotta do this and you gotta do that.

00:15:09.860 --> 00:15:16.440
You gotta like this. This, every, every puzzle feels like a lot of thought was put into it and, and there's like a satisfying answer.

00:15:16.780 --> 00:15:20.520
And there's also a joke to that, you know, like, yeah, it is satisfying when you solve them.

00:15:20.740 --> 00:15:30.740
But there are times where you can solve a puzzle and then screw it up in like the final minutes where you like accidentally push a block into a place that now you can't get to the door.

00:15:30.960 --> 00:15:32.200
You know? Yeah. I like that.

00:15:32.200 --> 00:15:40.260
You can never, yeah, I like that about it because you can never be like too confident and, you know, it's great that there's no time limit.

00:15:40.340 --> 00:15:42.760
Time limit would have killed this game for me personally.

00:15:42.760 --> 00:15:47.040
Oh, yeah. Because you really have to look at everything that's going on.

00:15:48.100 --> 00:15:50.860
And, you know, again, like that's why I was saying it was like feel good.

00:15:51.000 --> 00:15:51.940
Like everything's cute.

00:15:52.240 --> 00:16:02.060
There's never, you know, like other than those skulls that kind of like rush at you, there's really never any inherent danger as long as you're taking care of the whatever's at hand.

00:16:02.200 --> 00:16:04.120
You know? Those Medusa things too.

00:16:04.220 --> 00:16:06.780
Like as long as you've blocked them, they're not really a threat.

00:16:07.240 --> 00:16:09.100
So, I don't know.

00:16:09.220 --> 00:16:15.040
Like I appreciate that the game lets you go slow because ultimately this is a thinking game.

00:16:15.180 --> 00:16:17.240
This is not an action game.

00:16:17.760 --> 00:16:25.340
Right. Yeah. And even the skulls, I mean, while that is action-y and you're running from them and whatnot, you wake them up.

00:16:25.420 --> 00:16:26.780
Like your actions wake them up.

00:16:26.920 --> 00:16:30.880
They're usually just somewhere still on the level until you do something.

00:16:30.880 --> 00:16:33.900
Usually it's collecting like a certain number of hearts or whatever.

00:16:34.200 --> 00:16:36.440
But you wake them up.

00:16:36.580 --> 00:16:43.460
So, even that, you think about, okay, where do I want to be when I wake them up so I can get to the pearl before they get to me?

00:16:43.660 --> 00:16:45.300
And that sounds really simple.

00:16:45.300 --> 00:16:54.160
But sometimes it's actually like pretty complex like, because not to get like way into the nitty gritty, but like sometimes there are tiles you can only go over in one direction.

00:16:54.160 --> 00:16:56.020
So, once you cross this way, you can't go back.

00:16:56.100 --> 00:16:59.260
And you got to think about how can I loop around and be far enough away from these skulls?

00:16:59.300 --> 00:17:01.400
And how can I get them somewhere where they're not going to catch me?

00:17:01.400 --> 00:17:03.420
It's a lot to think about.

00:17:03.620 --> 00:17:05.220
Even the action-y parts are puzzly.

00:17:05.700 --> 00:17:16.600
Yeah. There's this like micro-planning moments where you have your main plan of what you're going to do and then how you're going to accomplish each part of that too.

00:17:16.960 --> 00:17:18.760
It's like a puzzle within a puzzle.

00:17:20.120 --> 00:17:32.620
This conversation sort of reminds me specifically when you're talking about the how you don't like the skulls are reacting to you.

00:17:32.740 --> 00:17:34.680
Like you control when the skulls do their thing.

00:17:34.860 --> 00:17:41.840
And it reminded me of a video I watched by Mark Brown of the Game Maker's Toolkit.

00:17:42.000 --> 00:17:48.400
And I think it's sort of like one of the defining characteristics of a puzzle game.

00:17:48.400 --> 00:17:51.540
And it's that you have perfect information.

00:17:51.540 --> 00:17:58.300
Like you may need to like experiment a little bit to understand the mechanics of a certain thing.

00:17:58.660 --> 00:18:09.320
But when you look at the screen, it might take you some time to fully comprehend everything that's going on.

00:18:09.400 --> 00:18:13.280
But all you need to know is what's on the screen.

00:18:13.280 --> 00:18:18.700
And I guess that's just me describing what a puzzle game is.

00:18:18.900 --> 00:18:21.640
