And then the Eggerland series, remember that Japanese one?

00:33:20.060 --> 00:33:25.420
That continued for a while, all the way to a game called Eggerland for Windows 95.

00:33:26.120 --> 00:33:27.940
And get ready for this, Joe.

00:33:28.360 --> 00:33:37.440
Eggerland for Windows 95 allows players to create their own Eggerland maps with a level creation program known as Construct Mode.

00:33:37.440 --> 00:33:48.400
So if you were having fun with Wrecking Crew way back in 85, you're going to love 1995 with Eggerland for Windows 95.

00:33:48.520 --> 00:33:49.640
Yeah, that sounds awesome.

00:33:49.640 --> 00:33:54.180
Although I think it might just like bring to light how much work goes into a puzzle game.

00:33:54.240 --> 00:34:01.040
Because I feel like if I were trying to make a puzzle, you know, in this style, it would take me a long time to make some good ones.

00:34:01.040 --> 00:34:07.200
So I think we've run the course on what we can say about The Adventures of Lolo, except for one little thing.

00:34:07.400 --> 00:34:09.960
There's one other thing we can say about The Adventures of Lolo.

00:34:10.200 --> 00:34:16.180
And that is whether it is an essential game or not on a little thing we like to call the Essential Games List.

00:34:19.440 --> 00:34:21.700
Sam, take me through your vote.

00:34:22.500 --> 00:34:24.940
All right. I think that this game is super charming.

00:34:25.360 --> 00:34:33.140
It has a very clear concept and like a very clear principle.

00:34:33.460 --> 00:34:35.960
And it sticks to that the entire time.

00:34:36.500 --> 00:34:39.840
And each part of it is really polished also.

00:34:40.060 --> 00:34:50.120
There's nothing that feels like an afterthought, which sometimes we see in games like this that have kind of not the same layout, but the same design on every floor.

00:34:50.120 --> 00:34:55.940
There's nothing that feels like just, all right, we'll just phone it in for this part of this level or anything.

00:34:56.120 --> 00:34:57.220
It all feels very intentional.

00:34:57.500 --> 00:35:04.200
If you want to play like a retro puzzle game, I feel like this is a perfect game to do it.

00:35:04.380 --> 00:35:08.340
And so for that, I see no reason why this shouldn't be an essential game.

00:35:08.500 --> 00:35:11.460
That is one vote for The Adventures of Lolo.

00:35:11.880 --> 00:35:13.140
Sean, your vote.

00:35:14.720 --> 00:35:17.380
Yeah, this was a huge surprise for me.

00:35:18.220 --> 00:35:20.220
I had never heard of this.

00:35:20.480 --> 00:35:28.880
I wasn't looking forward to this, but I really, really enjoyed this game.

00:35:29.440 --> 00:35:35.360
So without saying much more, I guess I'm just going to say that I also vote this game essential.

00:35:35.660 --> 00:35:39.620
Now that's really close to putting it on the Essential Games List.

00:35:39.980 --> 00:35:42.060
Joe, what are you going to do about it?

00:35:42.620 --> 00:35:46.800
All right. I love puzzle games in the first place.

00:35:46.920 --> 00:35:49.540
I didn't know this was a puzzle game going into it.

00:35:50.200 --> 00:35:59.300
But I think that really every level in this game feels like a satisfying puzzle, which so far isn't really something I think we've had yet on the NES.

00:35:59.440 --> 00:36:04.660
I think we've had games where even like Wrecking Crew, some of the puzzles were satisfying, but not all of them.

00:36:04.660 --> 00:36:12.920
And I think because of that and because of the combination of what's already been said, I think this is the first like really good puzzle game on the NES.

00:36:13.260 --> 00:36:21.720
Wrecking Crew is one of my personal favorites, but I think that this one actually like nails all of the positives down and gets it right.

00:36:22.360 --> 00:36:24.380
And I'm excited to go back to it.

00:36:24.660 --> 00:36:27.960
So, yeah, I'm with you guys.

00:36:28.120 --> 00:36:29.500
I'm going to vote at Essential.

00:36:29.500 --> 00:36:30.320
All right.

00:36:30.500 --> 00:36:32.420
And with that, it's on the Essential Games List.

00:36:32.560 --> 00:36:48.240
I have no power over the situation, but I will add that for me, when I'm playing these NES games, it's very noticeable how early in the game dev cycle like we are in terms of video games that I play.

00:36:48.400 --> 00:36:50.340
Because, like, you know, I play video games from all generations.

00:36:50.340 --> 00:36:56.780
So, when I play a video game today, I expect something a lot different than when we go back and play these NES games.

00:36:56.920 --> 00:37:01.880
Like, I'm able to suspend, you know, no different than when you watch an old film, right?

00:37:01.980 --> 00:37:03.340
Like a silent film or something like that.

00:37:03.440 --> 00:37:07.720
You have to be able to say, like, well, that's how it was back then and watch it with that in mind.

00:37:07.720 --> 00:37:09.800
And I have no problems doing that.

00:37:09.880 --> 00:37:15.720
But with a lot of these NES games, you run into a problem where they just didn't think it all the way through.

00:37:15.720 --> 00:37:20.100
They just kind of, like, created code for enemies to spawn in certain places.

00:37:20.260 --> 00:37:31.420
And they, you know, there's a difficulty spike that is just unreasonable or something happens that, you know, with the controls that, like, the character controls awkwardly.

00:37:32.120 --> 00:37:37.980
This has a lot of, like, things going for it because it's a puzzle game, right?

00:37:38.060 --> 00:37:42.020
So, they had to think of these puzzles in a certain way and work backwards from that.

00:37:42.020 --> 00:37:56.340
So, it's tough to say, like, you know, oh, you know, like, those action games from the NES, like, they kind of stink because they weren't thinking about, like, where enemy placement should be and how the character should move in all situations.

00:37:56.340 --> 00:37:58.400
And it's much easier to do that in a puzzle game.

00:37:59.620 --> 00:38:15.440
But it's worth noting that this game feels incredibly polished and, like, the game, you know, the developers actually took the time to go through each level and solve it backwards and make an entertaining game that doesn't have, like, one solution.

00:38:15.440 --> 00:38:18.020
That everybody must solve it the same exact way.

00:38:18.020 --> 00:38:26.300
There are plenty of ways to get stuck, but it's not like you have to play it a certain way and that, like, you just read a wiki when you get stuck or something like that.

00:38:26.380 --> 00:38:28.760
Like, you can totally figure these puzzles out.

00:38:29.140 --> 00:38:39.280
And so, I think that's what's going to put it on the Essential Games list for me is that it just feels like it's a step beyond most games we play on the NES right now.

00:38:39.280 --> 00:38:43.900
You know, who knows where we'll net out, if other developers will catch up or not.

00:38:44.020 --> 00:38:53.960
But I would say that Hal really did something incredible here because, well, the gameplay loop might fall stale after 50 levels of the same thing over and over again.

00:38:54.020 --> 00:39:04.360
You really have to appreciate the amount of, you know, thought they put into the levels and the overall, like, appearance and control of the game.

00:39:04.500 --> 00:39:06.740
So, absolutely, for me, it's an essential game.

00:39:07.180 --> 00:39:10.240
Hell yeah. That's four for four.

00:39:10.600 --> 00:39:12.120
It happens pretty often these days.

00:39:12.160 --> 00:39:16.660
We don't meet up beforehand to discuss these things, if anybody's wondering.

00:39:16.660 --> 00:39:23.880
I know that there's a spread in Las Vegas for whether the current episodes of Nostalgia will be essential or not.

00:39:23.980 --> 00:39:30.760
And for that reason, we are always investigated by the SEC to make sure that we are not – why would this stop?

00:39:30.940 --> 00:39:32.260
Why would they be investigating us?

00:39:32.800 --> 00:39:36.660
We also get calls from OSHA a lot for some reason.

00:39:36.660 --> 00:39:37.780
I'm not really sure why.

00:39:39.280 --> 00:39:40.540
Yeah, but –

00:39:40.540 --> 00:39:41.900
I'm not wearing a helmet, so.

00:39:43.020 --> 00:39:53.140
I say that it happens pretty often that we all vote essential, but that's not necessarily true because Joe and Sam voted essential for Marble Madness and me and Sean turned it down.

00:39:53.500 --> 00:39:56.660
And Joe turned down Tecmo Bowl, but we stopped him anyway.

00:39:56.860 --> 00:40:00.740
And I voted Jairus essential and you guys are, like, still laughing.

00:40:00.740 --> 00:40:03.460
Well, let's not forget about Zelda 2.

00:40:04.140 --> 00:40:05.400
Yeah. Oh, yeah.

00:40:05.820 --> 00:40:06.660
Did that come out?

00:40:06.900 --> 00:40:09.080
It still hurts. It came out?

00:40:09.360 --> 00:40:10.780
Yeah, you don't even remember it.

00:40:11.520 --> 00:40:14.020
You repressed the memory of your biggest mistake.

00:40:14.020 --> 00:40:18.780
Well, I'll tell you, there's a game coming out for the NES every Friday.

00:40:19.040 --> 00:40:21.900
No, not brand new games, but episodes of Nostalgia.

00:40:22.160 --> 00:40:29.760
And next week we have Omegon, which has probably the craziest box art for an NES game we've seen thus far.

00:40:29.760 --> 00:40:30.600
Don't worry.

00:40:30.920 --> 00:40:32.540
We'll definitely talk about it on the episode.

00:40:32.660 --> 00:40:38.720
But if you want to get a sneak peek, just go look at the Omegon box art on Google or something if you know about Google.

00:40:39.440 --> 00:40:41.360
And I think, like, that's just crazy.

00:40:42.240 --> 00:40:44.900
I don't know what kind of game we're going to get there, but I'm excited to play it.

00:40:45.040 --> 00:40:46.840
I'm excited to play all these games, good and bad.

00:40:47.020 --> 00:40:48.480
The games don't have to be essential.

00:40:48.840 --> 00:40:53.700
I've had a great time playing, you know, Star Soldier.

00:40:53.980 --> 00:40:55.680
I don't know. That's just, like, the first thing that came to my head.

00:40:55.780 --> 00:40:57.220
But, like, I had a good time playing that game.

00:40:57.340 --> 00:40:58.580
It didn't get on the essential games list.

00:40:58.580 --> 00:41:01.240
But, like, that's the fun of going back and discovering these old games.

00:41:01.320 --> 00:41:03.620
And that's why we hope you guys play along with us.

00:41:03.760 --> 00:41:08.060
You, the listeners, should absolutely, you know, see these games.

00:41:08.140 --> 00:41:14.560
And if you want to see which games are coming up next, don't wait until the previous episode releases and then you have only one week to play them.

00:41:14.800 --> 00:41:20.640
On our Twitter, at NostalgiaCast, I have a pinned post that says the next four games that are coming.

00:41:20.840 --> 00:41:25.740
That's right. You can play up to four weeks in advance and you're going to need it for some of these RPGs.

00:41:26.120 --> 00:41:28.340
I know we got Dragon Warrior coming up.

00:41:28.580 --> 00:41:30.840
We've got Mega Man 2.

00:41:31.120 --> 00:41:33.120
We've got Teenage Mutant Ninja Turtles.

00:41:33.400 --> 00:41:35.780
We've got a game called Mystery Quest.

00:41:35.980 --> 00:41:37.280
That sounds like a mystery to me.

00:41:38.340 --> 00:41:42.440
All right. That's all the time we have for this episode of Nostalgia.

